			<div class="content" id="post-content-4851651">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Some&nbsp;additional&nbsp;thoughts&nbsp;for&nbsp;you</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I do have to admit, once i finished the writing i read it about fifty times and every time there’s something that felt out of place or unnecessary but I didn’t know how to approach it. ( got scared to trim it.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>IMO: just trim it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The central core or what i’m really trying to say through the article would be learning who you really are or having peace with you inner conflict and finding power from it. Once the inner peace is achieved no one can really stop you or tell you that your wrong, but the society as a whole always finds a loop hole on the idea. So I guess i didn’t really made it clear.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think the central problem with the article, in terms of achieving this at the moment, is that this journey to find peace with your inner conflict isn't actually what the story is about? Like, the Foundation swoops in after it happens, so this like, intimate, personal struggle isn't shown; and instead we have this kinda, overview of what happened as a result of it happening?</p>
<p>My suggestion would be to do one of two things: either make this a tale, or start this sooner in the story.</p>
<p>In the first, you'd be radically changing the PoV so it'd be from SCP-XXXX-1's perspective, and entirely centred around them being put in a place where they're forced to find the peace within them, or in a place where finding that peace is their entire struggle, and the story would unfold as they gradually begin to find it while stuff around them gets worse; and the resolution would likely be when the Foundation comes in an interferes or something.</p>
<p>In the second, the focus would be the Foundation locking up -1 just as he's starting to gain this inner power, and so the central conflict would be around this boy trying to achieve the peace he needs whilst the Foundation are doing everything that they can to stop it: the ending would likely be the boy escaping to form the cave and the peaceful area, instead of that being the start. Like pacing would probably look something like &quot;Foundation grabs minor reality bender; reality bender gets stronger; Foundation does stuff to try and stop this; bender keeps getting more powerful; bender escapes and creates a place of peace, as a mirror to the place of conflict that was the foundation for him&quot;.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

